## What this session (sess388) established

**Answering your jab directly:** the ledger went 51→53, and that's two different things. Two of those entries are *newly-proven roots* I extracted from inside the #361/AGI symptom this session — RULE 6 requires ledgering a proven credible defect, so the diagnosis got more honest, not worse. But one of my fixes (0.19.37) genuinely regressed: it wedged nodes and cascaded to a fleet-wide quarantine. I reverted it. That part is on me, and I'm not dressing it up.

**Real wins, measured and verified:**
- **#474 (the AIL-freeze / relfence node-death that fired every lap) — root PROVEN and FIXED.** Kernel-stack + disassembly + a purpose-built lock-forensics probe showed the nonblocking AG acquire was *parking* on `pag_dlm_acquire_lock` (held by a sibling thread across its entire multi-second CAW poll) while holding ILOCK. Fix: `mutex_trylock` + `-EAGAIN` so the caller drops its locks. Clean laps now show **zero relfence wedges**.
- **The rsync_paired lap-2+ pace failure — root PROVEN by A/B.** The 50 GiB rig LUN formats to only 25 AGs, so `node_slot % agcount` makes 14 nodes share AGs pairwise — and those 14 were *exactly* the slow/failing set. I grew the LUN to 128 GiB (→ 64 AGs, no lock-code change) and **all 32 nodes now finish rsync_paired in 14–27s (PASS) across laps 1–3**, where 25 AGs failed with 11 nodes never finishing. posix_multi and dir_reuse_coherency also PASS every lap.

**Refuted and reverted:** a keep-dirty guard in `xfs_iflush` (0.19.36/37) — kept an unlanded obligation dirty but with *no converter able to write it* (held at PR, not EX), so it just relocated the wedge and cascaded. Exactly the hazard GPT's ruling predicted. Tree is back to the good state (0.19.38 ≡ 0.19.35).

## The remaining blocker
`dirent_durability` on an aged FS still FAILs — but the "lost" entries are all present on live re-check, so it's **late publication (>4s visibility), not data loss**. It shares one root with the fossil-`di_next_unlinked` shutdown: the removal (`di_next_unlinked=NULLAGINO`) never lands on the home block before reuse/authority release. The correct fix (GPT-ruled) is a **durability gate — a sanctioned converter pinned to the authority-release fence**, plus a store-at-iget overlay. That's real work, staged for the next session, and it's why I did not write YES.

Everything above is in the ledger and ccmemory (`ccloop-c7ee71c6-sess388-*`); the cluster is healthy at 0.19.38 / 64 AGs. **MXFS is not production ready: 53 open defects, publication durability unfixed.**
Criteria not met — continuing. Next angle per the GPT ruling: build fix (ii) first — consult the committed-next store at iget so a reused/evicted inode never loads a fossil `di_next_unlinked`. Let me find the store API and the iget load site.
The store is AG-tenure-scoped and purged at AG release, so a plain iget consult is fragile. The higher-value, lower-risk target is the exact shutdown site: `xfs_iunlink_log_inode` returning `-EFSCORRUPTED` when `i_next_unlinked` is a fossil. `mxfs_iunl_store_fossil_match` was built (sess203) precisely to distinguish a fossil from a real chain there. Checking whether it's wired at the LOGSAME site.
The precommit backstop exists but fires on a different path; the LOGSAME shutdown at `xfs_iunlink_log_inode:265` fires in the INSERT path before any item is created. Let me confirm all callers so I scope the fix precisely.
The remove path uses NULLAGINO (can't trip it); scrub is single-node. Only the insert-path call (line 702) hits the fossil false-positive. Let me confirm `mp`/single-node check are in scope in that function.
`mp` and the single-node helper are in scope in `xfs_iunlink_insert_inode`. Implementing the fossil reset immediately before the LOGSAME-tripping call.

